WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.967
You push your code to GitHub, and a minute later, a green tick appears next to your commit.

2
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.286
So where did your tests actually run?

3
00:00:10.286 --> 00:00:11.392
Not on your laptop.

4
00:00:11.392 --> 00:00:15.407
And how did a machine you have never seen know whether a test failed?

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.682
Hold on to both questions.

6
00:00:17.682 --> 00:00:19.283
First, a small cafe.

7
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:23.436
The owner tastes every pot of soup herself, before it goes out.

8
00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:25.389
On a quiet Tuesday, that works.

9
00:00:26.933 --> 00:00:31.081
On a busy Saturday, with a queue at the door, she skips one taste.

10
00:00:31.081 --> 00:00:32.024
Just this once.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.148
That is the pot with no salt.

12
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.232
Nobody would call her lazy.

13
00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:38.568
Her tongue is fine.

14
00:00:38.568 --> 00:00:40.537
Her Saturday is the problem.

15
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:46.228
A check that lives in a busy person's memory gets skipped exactly when it matters most.

16
00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:49.323
Testing on your own laptop has the same weakness.

17
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:52.545
What we want is a taster who has no Saturdays.

18
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:54.617
So let's hire one.

19
00:00:54.617 --> 00:00:59.475
Picture a restaurant kitchen, and follow one order through it, step by step.

20
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:00.266
You push.

21
00:01:00.266 --> 00:01:04.461
GitHub records an event: a push of this commit, to this branch.

22
00:01:04.461 --> 00:01:07.324
That's an order ticket landing on the rail.

23
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:16.220
GitHub opens the folder .github/workflows/ in the commit you pushed, and starts a run of every workflow that listens for push.

24
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.290
A workflow is just a text file that says what to run, and when.

25
00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.451
It is what's written on the ticket.

26
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.606
For each job, GitHub starts a brand new virtual machine, a computer simulated in software.

27
00:01:28.606 --> 00:01:30.208
It is called the runner.

28
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.422
It comes with Linux and common tools, but none of your files.

29
00:01:34.422 --> 00:01:35.939
A clean, empty station.

30
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:40.831
A program on that machine runs the job's steps, one at a time, in order.

31
00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:43.571
The cook follows the ticket, line by line.

32
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:47.637
The result, a green tick or a red cross, lands on the commit.

33
00:01:47.637 --> 00:01:49.684
Then GitHub deletes the machine.

34
00:01:49.684 --> 00:01:52.244
The station is wiped for the next order.

35
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.457
So your laptop takes no part, and no file survives from one job to the next.

36
00:01:57.457 --> 00:01:59.453
Your first question, answered.

37
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.802
And two jobs get two stations, side by side, at the same time.

38
00:02:03.802 --> 00:02:05.971
Like two cooks with two tickets.

39
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:09.446
Now let's write the job the obvious way.

40
00:02:09.446 --> 00:02:13.056
Install the packages the tests need, then run the tests.

41
00:02:13.056 --> 00:02:15.441
Exactly what you type on your laptop.

42
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.007
Pause and guess.

43
00:02:17.007 --> 00:02:18.237
Green, or red?

44
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.506
Red, at the very first step.

45
00:02:22.506 --> 00:02:26.417
requirements-dev.txt does not exist on that machine.

46
00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:28.975
On your laptop it was right there.

47
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:29.840
Of course.

48
00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.797
We gave the cook a spotless station, and no ingredients.

49
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:40.616
So nearly every job begins with actions/checkout, which copies your repository onto the machine.

50
00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:42.565
Fetch the ingredients first.

51
00:02:42.733 --> 00:02:46.857
Then setup-python puts Python 3.14 on the station.

52
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.267
Notice there are two kinds of step.

53
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.663
run executes your own shell commands.

54
00:02:51.663 --> 00:02:57.687
uses calls an action: ready made code that someone published, at a version after the at sign.

55
00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:00.016
Now the second question.

56
00:03:00.016 --> 00:03:03.968
How does a machine that never read your tests know that one failed?

57
00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:07.399
Every command ends with a number, its exit code.

58
00:03:07.399 --> 00:03:08.691
Zero means success.

59
00:03:08.691 --> 00:03:10.596
Anything else means trouble.

60
00:03:10.733 --> 00:03:13.492
pytest exits with 1 when a test fails.

61
00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:16.864
A non zero exit code fails the step.

62
00:03:16.864 --> 00:03:20.859
The job stops there and is marked failed, and so is the run.

63
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.038
So the machine doesn't read your test output at all.

64
00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.361
It reads one number.

65
00:03:26.361 --> 00:03:32.180
Most of CI rests on that habit: tools exit with a non zero code when something is wrong.

66
00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:37.553
A clean station is a fine taster, as long as you respect three rules.

67
00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:38.008
One.

68
00:03:38.008 --> 00:03:41.515
A self-hosted runner is a machine you run yourself.

69
00:03:41.515 --> 00:03:44.266
Never attach one to a public repository.

70
00:03:44.266 --> 00:03:52.723
Anyone can open a pull request, and a stranger's code could run on your machine, and leave things behind for your next job.

71
00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:54.855
A dirty station nobody cleaned.

72
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:55.321
Two.

73
00:03:55.321 --> 00:03:58.052
A job may run for at most 6 hours.

74
00:03:58.052 --> 00:04:03.916
Set timeout-minutes much lower, or a hung job waits the full 360 minutes.

75
00:04:04.067 --> 00:04:04.518
Three.

76
00:04:04.518 --> 00:04:11.887
The wiped station forgets everything, so every run downloads and installs the same packages again.

77
00:04:11.887 --> 00:04:14.519
Day 25 adds a cache that remembers.

78
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.164
So here's the whole story.

79
00:04:17.164 --> 00:04:18.453
A push is a ticket.

80
00:04:18.453 --> 00:04:20.421
A workflow says what to cook.

81
00:04:20.421 --> 00:04:23.000
A fresh runner cooks it, step by step.

82
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.375
And one exit code says how it went.

83
00:04:25.533 --> 00:04:27.673
A taster with no Saturdays.

84
00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:28.838
Quick check.

85
00:04:28.838 --> 00:04:34.263
You save your file as .github/workflow/ci.yml, with no s, and push.

86
00:04:34.263 --> 00:04:35.558
What do you see?

87
00:04:35.558 --> 00:04:36.853
Pause and think.

88
00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.017
Nothing at all.

89
00:04:40.017 --> 00:04:44.896
No run, no error, no warning, because GitHub never looks in that folder.

90
00:04:44.896 --> 00:04:48.556
If no run appears after a push, check that path first.
